WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.550 --> 00:00:10.720
I'm often drawn to
very generic materials

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:12.310
as a starting
point for my work I

00:00:12.310 --> 00:00:16.750
think because they
have a very easy entry

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:21.760
point things feel immediately
identifiable in some way.

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:24.280
There's a material resonance.

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:28.270
I think that people feel
that they understand

00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:35.530
what it is and in a way that
easy entry then I feel opens up

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:38.530
the possibility of
metaphor is kind

00:00:38.530 --> 00:00:41.200
of expanding out from them.

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:45.370
The first time I started
using the materials

00:00:45.370 --> 00:00:50.230
that are in this exhibition, the
pieces that look like figures.

00:00:50.230 --> 00:00:52.960
I call these forms
slumps because they're

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:54.670
sort of these
figurative forms that

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:59.440
depend on a hard
structure of some kind

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:02.080
to support them and armature.

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:06.640
And their way of thinking about
the relation between the body

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:10.390
and the support
systems that hold up

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:14.860
the body as a kind of
co-dependent relationship.

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:20.980
And I was particularly
interested in foam

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:24.520
as the base material
for these slumps

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:30.370
because it is a material that's
used it's a cheap bed material.

00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:32.560
The other material in
this exhibition of course

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:35.620
is are the emergency blankets.

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:38.590
They are of course,
materials that

00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:42.290
are used to keep people warm.

00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:46.390
It has a protective
element to it.

00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:49.300
But when stretched,
it also turns

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:52.060
into a kind of two way mirror.

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:55.690
I'm interested in
that and how as people

00:01:55.690 --> 00:02:00.490
walk in front of and
behind this vertical plane,

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:03.790
they are hidden and
revealed from each other.

00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:06.700
They are reflected
again into the space

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:10.389
and sometimes into each other
because it's a two way mirror

00:02:10.389 --> 00:02:14.140
And it also presents
the sculpture

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:17.830
through reflection
in this rounded form.

00:02:17.830 --> 00:02:21.850
So there are a variety of
formal and conceptual reasons

00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:26.950
that are always working
together in my material choices.

00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:31.840
And I'm always thinking
about these different layers

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:34.180
of the space vertically.

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:41.860
And in this exhibition, there
are hanging water bottles

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:44.230
that are sort of at mid-level.

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:46.720
They're kind of at eye level.

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:52.180
There are these, I think of
them as extensions of the body.

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:54.910
They're water
bottles so they are

00:02:54.910 --> 00:02:56.920
used to carry water
which you drink

00:02:56.920 --> 00:03:01.180
to replenish your own water.

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:03.670
But I've also been
interested in how

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:07.840
day laborers and people
who, again don't have space,

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:10.900
don't have a bathroom
to go to often will use

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:13.760
that same bottle to urinate in.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:16.930
And so it becomes this
receptacle for bodily fluids

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:18.720
as well.

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:21.430
And so in a way,
it's a size of a head

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:22.870
and it hangs at head height.

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:27.250
So I think of them as
again bodily at the height

00:03:27.250 --> 00:03:29.830
of a person standing.

00:03:29.830 --> 00:03:32.170
And then finally, these
exit signs or pieces

00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:35.200
that are quite high up on
the wall and force people

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:36.850
to look up.

00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:41.300
They're also hung in a way
where from any given point,

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:45.580
you can at best see one
side of an exit sign

00:03:45.580 --> 00:03:49.090
and so you have to move through
the space to see the two

00:03:49.090 --> 00:03:50.890
words on any given sign.

00:03:50.890 --> 00:03:54.640
And so the movement
of the viewer

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:56.650
is an important
part of the piece

00:03:56.650 --> 00:04:02.500
things are hidden and revealed
as you walk through the space.

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:05.050
In this current show
and in previous shows,

00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:07.750
I used text in
different ways here.

00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:10.750
It's through these what
I think of as two word

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:16.269
poems in a way, these
exit signs that I think

00:04:16.269 --> 00:04:22.120
help people
contextualize the work,

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:25.840
gives people anchors through
which to enter the work

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:31.360
rather than provide a
descriptive entry point.

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:35.020
So for example, let's see.

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:38.420
Grave/ Grove, the title
of the exhibition,

00:04:38.420 --> 00:04:45.010
which is also an exit sign
in the exhibition was a way

00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:51.350
of thinking about this moment
that we're living through,

00:04:51.350 --> 00:04:57.170
the pandemic as a moment that
is of course one that reminds us

00:04:57.170 --> 00:04:58.670
of our mortality.

00:04:58.670 --> 00:05:01.400
We're confronted
constantly with death.

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:04.760
But last spring,
there was this moment

00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:10.400
when the seasons changed
in New York and plants

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:14.300
started to grow out
of the sidewalk cracks

00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:16.100
and there was something
quite beautiful

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:20.810
about this rebirth
within a city.

00:05:20.810 --> 00:05:25.520
A rebirth not so much of the
people but of this other life

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:28.040
form these plants
and particularly

00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:31.910
plants that are not
considered valuable to humans

00:05:31.910 --> 00:05:33.170
that are considered weeds.

00:05:33.170 --> 00:05:38.030
But that irrespective of our
desire to have them around,

00:05:38.030 --> 00:05:42.040
they thrive and they
come back every year.

